WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.005
Least privilege, at every layer.

2
00:00:02.285 --> 00:00:07.107
Least privilege means each piece holds the narrowest access that does its job.

3
00:00:07.387 --> 00:00:10.032
For MCP it applies at five layers.

4
00:00:10.312 --> 00:00:11.165
Tools.

5
00:00:11.445 --> 00:00:16.885
Prefer update_issue_status with an enum and a transition table over run_sql.

6
00:00:17.165 --> 00:00:26.189
A tool that takes an arbitrary command, query, or path cannot be meaningfully reviewed, because what it does depends entirely on its arguments.

7
00:00:26.469 --> 00:00:27.472
Credentials.

8
00:00:27.752 --> 00:00:44.883
The local-server guide says to pass secrets per server through the host’s configuration, never as globally exported shell variables, and to give each server its own narrowly scoped token, such as a fine-grained token limited to one repository rather than your account-wide one.

9
00:00:45.163 --> 00:00:46.144
Filesystem.

10
00:00:46.424 --> 00:00:49.688
Grant one project directory, read-only where possible.

11
00:00:49.968 --> 00:00:58.096
The guide warns that configured allowlists are cooperative boundaries: an honest server respects them, a malicious one ignores them.

12
00:00:58.376 --> 00:01:05.715
Enforcement comes from isolation, such as a container where the allowed directory is the only filesystem that exists.

13
00:01:05.995 --> 00:01:06.805
Network.

14
00:01:07.085 --> 00:01:12.824
A server that works on local files needs no network at all; with a container that is one flag.

15
00:01:13.104 --> 00:01:17.072
A server that wraps one API should reach only that API’s hosts.

16
00:01:17.352 --> 00:01:19.251
Exfiltration needs an exit.

17
00:01:19.531 --> 00:01:20.661
OAuth scopes.

18
00:01:20.941 --> 00:01:32.547
For remote servers, the security best practices page recommends a minimal initial scope for low-risk reads and step-up requests for privileged operations when they are first attempted.

19
00:01:32.827 --> 00:01:41.531
It lists wildcard scopes and publishing every scope up front as common mistakes, because a stolen broad token can reach everything it covers.

20
00:01:41.811 --> 00:01:44.200
One fact about your own setup belongs here.

21
00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:52.693
Claude Code’s sandbox covers shell commands only; its docs say local MCP servers run outside it, with your full access.

22
00:01:52.973 --> 00:02:00.077
If you want a server isolated, isolate the server (or run the whole Claude Code process in a container or VM).
